      "content": "**Quick Start Guide to GridScale Deploy**\n\nTo get started with GridScale Deploy, follow these simple steps:\n\n1. **Install Required Tools**: Ensure Docker and kubectl are installed on your machine. Docker is a containerization platform that allows you to package and deploy applications in isolated environments, while kubectl is a command-line tool for managing Kubernetes clusters. You can download Docker and kubectl from their official websites.\n2. **Initialize GridScale Deploy**: Run `gs-deploy init` to create a new GridScale Deploy project. This command initializes a new directory with the necessary configuration files and sets up the project structure. You'll see a `gridscale-deploy.yml` file generated, which defines the deployment settings for your application.\n3. **Deploy to Production**: Use `gs-deploy up --env prod` to deploy your application to production. This command builds your Docker image, pushes it to a container registry, and deploys it to a Kubernetes cluster. The `--env prod` flag specifies that you want to deploy to a production environment, which will configure the deployment settings accordingly.\n\n**Important**: By default, GridScale Deploy listens on port 8080. You can verify that the deployment is successful by visiting `http://",
